> Hi Jakub,
>
> I understand your frustration. Let me explain. Kafka wants to divide u=
p=20
> all listeners into either broker listeners or controller listeners. Th=
e=20
> sets are disjoint: a listener can't be both.
>
> BrokerServer will try to open the ports that belong to the broker;=20
> ControllerServer will try to open the ports that belong to the=20
> controller. You obviously can't open the same port twice (in standard=20
> UNIX, at least) or if you somehow did, the result would be nonsense.=20
> This isn't such a big deal on broker or controller nodes, but it=20
> becomes a bigger deal when in "combined" mode, where a process=20
> functions as both broker and controller.
>
> You are getting the error message "requirement failed:=20
> control.plane.listener.name must be a listener name defined in=20
> advertised.listeners" because the broker is looking for a listener=20
> named CONTROLPLANE-9090, but it already knows that this is NOT an=20
> advertised listener for the broker, it is an advertised listener for=20
> the controller. You would get the same error if you tried to set the=20
> inter.broker.listener or the broker replication listener to the=20
> CONTROLLER listener.
>
> The reason why this wasn't an issue in 3.8.0, and is in 3.9-RC2 is tha=
t=20
> in 3.8.0 and earlier, advertised.listeners was just for broker=20
> listeners. There was never a need to put a controller listener in ther=
e=20
> because controller listeners were statically configured by=20
> controller.quorum.voters. In fact, when in KRaft mode, it would be a=20
> fatal configuration error to put a controller listener into=20
> advertised.listeners. Unfortunately, we failed to enforce this when th=
e=20
> broker was in migration mode.
>
> I guess for the purpose of being bug-compatible with 3.8, we could mak=
e=20
> an exception here and force the listener specified by=20
> control.plane.listener to appear in effectiveAdvertisedBrokerListeners=
.=20
> Since control plane listeners are going away anyway, we won't have to=20
> support this exception for very long.
>
> By the way, I wasn't trying to be dismissive of your bug report (in=20
> fact I spent several hours on it). I felt (in fact I still feel) that=20
> it's a configuration error. But your compatibility argument is a=20
> reasonable one. So let's be compatible.
>
> best,
> Colin
>
>>
